This repository is licensed in TWO PARTS, split along what the code is for.
Every published package states its own license in its manifest, and every tree
below carries a copy of the license that governs it. This file only says which
is which.

## The seam is BUSL-1.1 - see LICENSE-BUSL

    c/magicseam
    go/magicseam
    rust/seamwire
    ts/magicseam
    zig/magicseam

These implement the magic-seam wire protocol itself - the opcodes, the
capability negotiation, the mTLS QUIC transport. rust/seamwire is in this list
despite its name: it IS the magic-seam vocabulary, shared by trail and the comet
agent rather than by guests.

Change Date and Additional Use Grant are in LICENSE-BUSL; NOTICE summarises
them.

## The guest side is Apache-2.0 - see LICENSE-APACHE

    c/periapsis
    go/gazer
    rust/perseid
    ts/periapsis

These are what a program you write links against to run ON Periapsis - the
periapsis:* WASI interfaces, the Perseid vocabulary, the aperture expression
language. A guest SDK that restricted its own consumers would defeat its
purpose, so this half is permissive.

go/gazer joined them on 2026-09-02 and is the one that was not always here. It
is the Gazer identity vocabulary - a naming convention, its parser and its
builder, stdlib only - and it left go/magicseam because periapsis-CE is GPLv3
and depended on exactly those four symbols. BUSL's production-use conditions and
GPLv3 section 10 cannot both hold for a downstream recipient, so the half that
never touched the seam became permissive. go/magicseam keeps GazerPrincipal,
which needs the seam's own Caller type, and imports go/gazer: the dependency
runs BUSL -> Apache and must never run the other way.
